.SH NAME
bssrecv \- Bundle Streaming Service reception test program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR \fImenu_option\fR \fIparameters\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR uses BSS to acquire streaming data from \fBbssStreamingApp\fR.
.PP
\&\fBbssrecv\fR is a menu-driven interactive test program, run from the operating
system shell prompt.  The program enables the user to begin and end a session
of BSS data acquisition from \fBbssStreamingApp\fR, displaying the data as it
arrives in real time; to replay data acquired during the current session;
and to replay data acquired during a prior session.
.PP
If no argument is provided, a list of menu option is shown on shell prompt.
.PP
If only the \fImenu_option\fR is specified, additional prompt, when necessary,
will be displayed to request user input for the necessary parameters.
.PP
If both \fImenu_option\fR and \fIparameters\fR are specified, bssrecv will directly
execute the specified action.
.PP
When initiating the acquisition or replay of data from \fBbssStreamingApp\fR, 
which corresponds to menu option 1, 2 and 3, the user must provide the following
three additional parameters:
.IP "BSS database name" 4
.IX Item "BSS database name"
All data acquired by the BSS session thread will be written to a BSS "database"
comprising three files: table, list, and data.  The name of the database
is the root name that is common to the three files, e.g., \fIdb3\fR.tbl,
\&\fIdb3\fR.lst, \fIdb3\fR.dat would be the three files making up the \fIdb3\fR BSS
database.
.IP "path name" 4
.IX Item "path name"
All three files of the selected BSS database must reside in the same
directory of the file system; the path name of that directory is required.
.IP "endpoint ID" 4
.IX Item "endpoint ID"
In order to acquire streaming data issued by \fBbssStreamingApp\fR, the \fBbssrecv\fR
session thread must open the BP endpoint to which that data is directed.  For
this purpose, the ID of that endpoint is needed.
.IP "fromTime & toTime" 4
.IX Item "fromTime & toTime"
In playback mode, you may specific specific time period starting from \fIfromTime\fR
to \fItoTime\fR as part of the command line argument. If these are required but 
missing, the user will be prompted to enter them. The time format is  
\&\fIyyyy/mm/dd\-hh:mm:ss\fR, where \fIyyyy\fR is the year, \fImm\fR is the month, \fIdd\fR is the
day of the month, \fIhh\fR is the hour, \fImm\fR is the minute, and \fIss\fR is the second.
.PP
\&\fBbssrecv\fR offers the following menu options:
.IP "1. Open BSS Receiver in playback mode" 4
.IX Item "1. Open BSS Receiver in playback mode"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR prompts the user for the three parameter values noted above, then
opens the indicated BSS database for replay of the data in that database.
.IP "2. Start BSS receiving thread in realtime mode only" 4
.IX Item "2. Start BSS receiving thread in realtime mode only"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR prompts the user for the three parameter values noted above, then
starts a background session thread to acquire data into the indicated database.
Each bundle that is acquired is passed to a display function that prints a
single line consisting of N consecutive '*' characters, where N is computed
as the data number at the start of the bundle's payload data, modulo 150.
Note that the database is \fBnot\fR open for replay at this time. To replay
the database, one should switch to mode 1 or mode 3.
.IP "3. Run BSS receiver thread in realtime mode (concurrently replay-capable)" 4
.IX Item "3. Run BSS receiver thread in realtime mode (concurrently replay-capable)"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR prompts the user for the three parameter values noted above, then
starts a background session thread to acquire data into the indicated database
(displaying the data as described for option 2 above) and also opens the
database so it is capable for replay. However, in the bssrecv.c program currently 
replay is not triggered. The user can modify bssrecv.c program to add a 
concurrent replay session.
.IP "4. Close current playback mode session" 4
.IX Item "4. Close current playback mode session"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR closes the indicated BSS database, terminating replay access.
.IP "5. Stop BSS receiving thread in realtime mode" 4
.IX Item "5. Stop BSS receiving thread in realtime mode"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR terminates the current background session thread.  Replay access
to the BSS database, if currently open, is \fBnot\fR terminated.
.IP "6. Stop BSS Receiver in both realtime and playback mode" 4
.IX Item "6. Stop BSS Receiver in both realtime and playback mode"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR terminates the current background session thread.  Replay access
to the BSS database, if currently open, is also terminated.
.IP "7. Exit" 4
.IX Item "7. Exit"
\&\fBbssrecv\fR terminates.
.Sp
For each streaming payload displayed, \fBbssrecv\fR prints a string of "*" 
characters, whose length is the sequence number modulo 150 and at the end
of the string, the sequence number, which is inserted as the first byte of 
the payload by the \fBbssStreamingApp\fR program, is printed out.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.IX Header "EXIT STATUS"
.IP """0""" 4
.IX Item """0"""
\&\fBbssrecv\fR has terminated.
